Nonprofit organizations often have a lot of material that they would
love to see widely distributed. Electronic distribution via computers
and networks should be perfect for them. The problem is that they are
unfamiliar with the technology.

In this article tells how to get the material on-line. The article
generalized my experience with three nonprofit organizations. The
organizations are American Library Association (ALA), the American
Association of University Professors (AAUP), and the American Civil
Liberties Union (ACLU).

TIP: Call them on the phone.

They will have many, many questions. Regular mail and fax is fine for
confirming what is decided on the phone, but the phone is the only
effective way to address their concerns.

TIP: Know their publications. Get a catalog or policy book.

The ALA has a policy book and and policy papers. The AAUP has a policy
book and publishes policy statements in their magazine. The ACLU has
"briefing papers" and some special flyers.

TIP: Call the organization's publications department.

They are the only ones who know how to authorize distribution.

TIP: Find out the status of the material. Ask if it is in the
public domain or if distribution requires permission.

AAUP policy statements are in the public domain. Other items in their
magazine are copyrighted. The ALA is vague about the status of their
material, but they clearly prefer that material be distributed with
their permission. ACLU material is copyrighted.

TIP: Don't ask them to allow everyone to freely distribute the
material. Instead ask them to give *you* permission to distribute the
material on, for example, Usenet and Internet archive (a.k.a. anonymous
ftp)

In a perfect world, non-profits would distribute their material as
freeware. In practice, most distribute it according to the traditions
of traditional publishing. This means that distribution permission is
granted to specific people, not to everyone.

TIP: Ask to distribute short material, such as policy statements,
rather than books.

They often distribute the paper versions of short material for free
anyway, so giving you permission to distribute electronic versions for
free is not much of a leap. Also, they are less likely to own
the copyright of books.

TIP: If you have a track record of distributing material, mention it.

You could also mention that the ALA, AAUP, and ACLU have authorized
electronic distribution of some of their materials.

TIP: Expect to type or scan the documents in.

Don't expect them to give you on-line copies. Don't even ask if they
have on-line copies until after you have distribution permission. (This
is also a good time to ask if they have email.)

TIP: Estimate the impact.

Even a medium-sized Usenet newsgroup reaches 100,000 people. Tell them
how many people their material will reach. (Usenet estimates are
posted every month in news.lists)

TIP: Emphasize that it is free, free, free.

They will have trouble believing that they will be able to reach
hundreds of thousands of people at no cost to them beyond the cost of
writing you a permission letter.

TIP: Suggest a trail run.

Suggestion a trail run in which they give you permission to distribute
some of the material and then both of you see how that goes.

TIP: After you distribute the material, follow up.

If they don't have on-line copies of their own material, send it to
them.  Also, if you can measure the actual impact, tell them.

- Carl


ANNOTATED REFERENCES

The document I've put on-line so far are available by anonymous ftp
(the preferred method) and by email. To get the README file(s) via
ftp, do an anonymous ftp to ftp.eff.org (192.77.172.4), and get
file(s):

  pub/academic/library/README
  pub/academic/civil-liberty/README
  pub/academic/academic/README

To get the README file(s) by email, send email to
archive-server@eff.org. Include the line(s) (be sure to include the
space before the file name):

send acad-freedom/library README
send acad-freedom/civil-liberty README
send acad-freedom/academic README
